Source: podman
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: The Wondersmith <the@wondersmith.dev>
# debhelper >= 13.13 is required, not just compat level 13: earlier versions
# (bookworm ships 13.11) discover systemd units by scanning only
# debian/<pkg>/lib/systemd/system, while this package installs to the
# DEP17-correct usr/lib/systemd/system. On those versions dh_installsystemd
# silently finds nothing and enables nothing.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), debhelper (>= 13.13~)
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Rules-Requires-Root: no

# TARGET SUITE: trixie, not bookworm.
#
# bookworm cannot satisfy this package's runtime dependencies at all:
#   crun     1.8.1   < 1.14 required
#   conmon   2.1.6   < 2.1.7 required
#   golang-github-containers-common  not present
# and its debhelper is too old for the unit discovery described above. trixie
# supplies crun 1.21, conmon 2.1.12 and golang-github-containers-common 0.62.
#
# Note that the dependency is golang-github-containers-common, which is what
# Debian actually calls it. `containers-common` is the Alpine and Fedora name
# and does not exist in Debian, not even as a virtual package.
#
# libsystemd0 is listed EXPLICITLY and must stay that way. podman reaches
# journald through go-systemd's sdjournal, which dlopens libsystemd.so.0 rather
# than linking it -- verified: the binary carries the sd_journal_* symbols and
# the "libsystemd.so.0" string, but no ELF NEEDED entry. dh_shlibdeps cannot see
# a dlopen, so ${shlibs:Depends} does not pick it up. Keeping the journald log
# driver working is the entire reason the Debian podman is a separate,
# dynamically linked build, so its dependency should not be left to chance
# (systemd hosts happen to always have it, which is exactly how this stays
# invisible until someone hits it).
#
# ca-certificates is likewise a hard dependency rather than a Recommends. Debian's
# own podman only recommends it, but this package has no Recommends by policy, and
# without a trust store podman cannot pull from any HTTPS registry:
#
#   tls: failed to verify certificate: x509: certificate signed by unknown authority
#
# A podman that cannot fetch an image is not usefully installed.
#
# container-network-stack MUST be in Provides, and is NOT a typo for netavark.
#
# Provides is not transitive. Claiming `netavark` does not also claim what the
# real netavark package claims. Debian's netavark declares
# `Provides: container-network-stack (= 2)` and containernetworking-plugins
# declares `container-network-stack (= 1)` -- the number is the stack
# generation (1 = CNI, 2 = netavark), not the provider's own version, which is
# why it is hardcoded rather than templated from @NETAVARK_VERSION@.
#
# golang-github-containers-common, which this package depends on, depends on
# container-network-stack -- UNVERSIONED, so a generation-1 provider formally
# satisfies it. Without the line below there are exactly two candidates, and
# NEITHER is a good outcome:
#
#   netavark                     Conflicts with this package, so never selected.
#   containernetworking-plugins  NOT conflicted here, and a perfectly
#                                installable trixie package (1.1.1+ds1-3).
#                                Nothing stops apt from pulling it in.
#
# The second one is the trap, and it is why this Provides is about correctness
# and not only about installability. containernetworking-plugins is CNI, the
# generation-1 stack podman removed support for in 5.0 -- podman 6.0 cannot use
# a single binary in it -- and it declares Depends: iptables, which this
# package deliberately avoids, netavark 2.x having deleted all iptables support
# (the reason nftables is a hard dependency above). Resolving that way installs
# a dead network stack plus the firewall backend this package exists to not
# need, while the stack actually shipped goes undeclared.
#
# In the reported failure apt 3.0 declined BOTH candidates and the install
# failed outright rather than resolving wrongly:
#
#   Unsatisfied dependencies:
#    golang-github-containers-common : Depends: container-network-stack
#   ...
#    - containernetworking-plugins:amd64=1.1.1+ds1-3+b17 is not selected for install
#    - netavark:amd64=1.14.0-2 is not selected for install because:
#        podman:amd64=6.0.2-2 Conflicts netavark
#
# apt gave a reason for rejecting netavark and NONE for rejecting
# containernetworking-plugins. Do not read a hard constraint into that silence:
# nothing in this control forbids it, and why it went unselected on that host
# was not established. The claim below is honest independently of any of this
# -- the bundled netavark @NETAVARK_VERSION@ at /usr/libexec/podman IS a
# generation-2 container network stack -- and declaring it makes the dependency
# resolve to the stack that is actually installed.

Package: podman
Architecture: @ARCH@
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
 crun (>= 1.14) | runc,
 conmon (>= 2.1.7),
 passt,
 nftables,
 golang-github-containers-common,
 uidmap,
 init-system-helpers (>= 1.54),
 libsystemd0,
 ca-certificates
Conflicts: netavark, aardvark-dns, catatonit, buildah
Replaces: netavark, aardvark-dns, catatonit, buildah
Provides: netavark (= @NETAVARK_VERSION@),
 aardvark-dns (= @AARDVARK_DNS_VERSION@),
 catatonit (= @CATATONIT_VERSION@),
 buildah (= @BUILDAH_VERSION@),
 container-network-stack (= 2)
Description: Self-contained Podman bundle (netavark, aardvark-dns, catatonit, buildah CLI)
 Podman @PODMAN_VERSION@ with every required helper binary bundled under
 /usr/libexec/podman: netavark @NETAVARK_VERSION@ (nftables-only),
 aardvark-dns @AARDVARK_DNS_VERSION@, catatonit @CATATONIT_VERSION@ (init),
 rootlessport, and quadlet. The standalone buildah @BUILDAH_VERSION@ CLI is
 also included at /usr/bin/buildah.
 .
 No external netavark, aardvark-dns, catatonit, or buildah package is
 required; all four are bundled and their virtual packages are provided here
 at the exact bundled versions, so a versioned dependency on any of them is
 satisfied by installing this package.
 .
 The bundled helpers are statically linked against musl and carry no runtime
 library dependencies of their own. Only the podman binary itself is
 dynamically linked, against glibc and libsystemd, so that the journald log
 driver remains available on systemd hosts.
 .
 Rootless networking is handled by passt/pasta; slirp4netns was removed in
 podman 6.0 and is not a dependency. nftables is required at runtime because
 netavark 2.0 deleted all iptables support. cgroups v2 is required; cgroups v1
 is not supported.
